<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emperor Shirakawa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1053–1129</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Shirakawa-tennō, Sadahito, Rokujō no Mikado</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emperor Shirakawa was the 72nd emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> according to the traditional order of succession, reigning from 1073 to 1087. The eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-go-sanjo">Emperor Go-Sanjō</a>, he tried like his father to rule directly, regulating the shōen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manorialism">manor system</a> and weakening the regents' lines. In 1087 he abdicated in favour of his son Horikawa but went on exercising broad powers from his residence, the Shirakawa-in, becoming the first to practise what came to be known as cloistered rule. In 1096 he entered the Buddhist priesthood. By his death in 1129 he had ruled as cloistered emperor through the reigns of three emperors who were effectively little more than figureheads.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-shirakawa.jpg" alt="Emperor Shirakawa" width="330" height="304" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emperor_Shirakawa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Shirakawa was born on 7 July 1053, the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-go-sanjo">Emperor Go-Sanjō</a> and Fujiwara Shigeko. His personal name was Sadahito, and in his youth his relations with his father were cold but loving. He was proclaimed an imperial prince in 1068, when his father was enthroned, and became crown prince in 1069. Go-Sanjō abdicated in the twelfth month of Enkyū 4 (January 1073), and Shirakawa acceded at the age of 19.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sessho-and-kampaku">kampaku</a> was in place, but Shirakawa tried to rule directly, as his father had. He attempted to regulate the shōen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manorialism">manor system</a> and to weaken the influence of the sekkan lines of regents. In 1077 he had the temple Hosshō-ji built at Shirakawa in fulfilment of a sacred vow. It was the first of a series of sacred-vow temples created by imperial decree, and its nine-storey pagoda, begun in 1083, became the most elaborate temple structure sponsored by an emperor up to that time.</p><p>In 1081 the monks of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-hiei">Mount Hiei</a> twice burned the temple of Miidera. When the empress Kenshi died in 1084, he was afflicted with great grief and for a time turned the administration over to his ministers. Go-Sanjō had wished Shirakawa's younger half-brother to succeed him, but the half-brother died of an illness in 1085, and Shirakawa's own son Taruhito became crown prince. On the day Taruhito was proclaimed heir, Shirakawa formally abdicated, on 3 January 1087, and took the title Daijō-tennō. Taruhito became Emperor Horikawa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The retired emperor was the first to attempt what became customary cloistered rule: he exercised power indirectly from the Shirakawa-in, the White River Mansion, although the nominal offices of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sessho-and-kampaku">sesshō</a> and kampaku continued to exist for a long time. In 1096, on the death of his daughter, he entered a monastery under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">Dharma</a> name Yūkan, becoming a hō-ō, the title of a former emperor who has become a monk.</p><p>After Horikawa's death his grandson became Emperor Toba, and Toba in turn abdicated to his son, who became Emperor Sutoku. By his death in 1129 Shirakawa had ruled as cloistered emperor through the reigns of three emperors who were effectively little more than figureheads. His posthumous name comes from the Shirakawa-in; another name for him was Rokujō no Mikado.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Emperor Shirakawa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Emperors too could take the robe: in 1087 <b>Emperor Shirakawa</b> abdicated and became a Buddhist monk, the first of the cloistered emperors.</blockquote><a href="/societies/heian-japan">Social Hierarchy of Heian Japan</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kyoto, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in Kyoto, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.01,135.77&amp;year=1102" aria-label="Kyoto on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Emperor Shirakawa"><image href="/maps/globes/g150n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4013 4510h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4013 4510h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.01,135.77&amp;year=1102">What was here in 1102? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
